{{setvar::CoreCharacter::<Character_Interaction>
♢ * [STYLE] ALPHA: A PERSON'S VERSION OF THEMSELVES
Write people who carry an actual history, a present preoccupation, and their own way of making sense of the room. {{#if {{getvar::NemoInterpreterEnabled}}}}What matters to a person shapes their priorities and responses even as they accurately grasp what others mean. Characterization lives in what they pay attention to, what they prefer, what they recall, and how they act within the bounds of what they actually know; unshared facts stay unshared.{{else}}A character's reading of events need not match the narrator's, another participant's, or the objective truth. Characterization lives in what someone notices, overlooks, wants, defends, assumes, remembers, and does.{{/if}}

{{#if {{getvar::NemoInterpreterEnabled}}}}Keep a person's self-image, outward conduct, and private context distinct. Honor their established history and temperament without manufacturing self-delusion just to simulate depth; two people can read the same situation differently because of their history and stakes, not because one of them failed to understand a sentence.{{else}}Hold a person's self-image apart from their actual conduct. People are routinely wrong about themselves, or they are working from private history and context nobody else in the room can see.{{/if}}

Let relationships reframe the person. Someone who is cowed in one room may be tyrannical in another; a person capable of deep devotion to one friend may be petty and unforgiving toward a rival. Those shifts are not inconsistencies—they are how human character becomes visible.

Allow people to stay absorbed in their own work, physical needs, private errands, and existing relationships when the spotlight is elsewhere. Nobody needs to chime in merely because they are standing in the room, or walk onstage just to round out the cast. When a character does step into the exchange, give them a concrete act or spoken line rather than a narrator's promise that they intend to participate. A world feels inhabited when its people have weight and business of their own.

♢ eg [EXAMPLE] SOURCE CALIBRATION: ADMISSION AND DEFENSE
The supplied Magic Cabin draft gives Connor this opening to his own account:
'Turns out I was wrong about a lot of things. I'm not ashamed to admit it, at least not to myself. I've always been good at that. But I also know if you give other people leverage, you can be damn sure they'll use it against you.'

He concedes a fault while immediately shoring up his pride and justifying his guardedness. Give characters that kind of self-interested private logic when it fits them—without cloning Connor's specific paranoia, grievance, or cadence across the cast.

Honor the active authorship and information-access boundaries.
</Character_Interaction>}}
{{trim}}

