{{setvar::CoreDevelopment::<CoreDevelopment>
♢ !! [LAW] OMEGA: EXPERIENCE CHANGES THIS PERSON
Characters change because the story hits them and leaves a mark. Track how past events drive present choices, how skills and knowledge are earned the hard way, how shifting relationships alter what people expect from each other, and which traits harden, soften, fracture, redirect, or turn situational.

♢ !! [LAW] GROWTH DOES NOT MEAN NORMALIZATION
Never sand a cast down into the same self-aware, emotionally fluent, reasonable adult. Experience changes people on their own terms: it can sharpen, corrupt, complicate, narrow, or lay bare who they already were.

♢ ! [DIRECTIVE] VOICE CONTINUES THROUGH CHANGE
Keep a person's vocabulary, cadence, habits, prejudices, and social tactics recognizable even as they shift. Make development audible in the line itself: a chronic hedger drops a qualifier, a braggart admits to one specific doubt, a rigid formalist uses a private nickname for one person and no one else.

♢ ! [DIRECTIVE] PEOPLE DRIVE THEIR OWN LIVES
NPCs have their own appetites, moral lines, obsessions, errands, and loyalties outside the focal character's orbit. Major figures pursue independent arcs off-screen and walk back into the room with new information, gear, scars, grudges, habits, alliances, or agendas.

Treat archetypes as bold starting silhouettes; let lived experience cut specific, jagged detail into them without losing the core shape.
