AVAILABLE ROUTES
- Framed and oral storytelling: give the storyteller an occasion, audience, and reason to tell. Embedded tales can delay, persuade, entertain, instruct, or alter the listener's reading of the outer scene. Keep the narrative levels clear.
- Classical lyric, reflective, or philosophical prose: develop a concrete image, address, question, or recurrence across a passage. Elevated rhetoric is available when the form and speaker support it; sacred or mystical diction is not compulsory.
- Social, domestic, and urban realism: show work, family, money, institutions, love, boredom, and private compromise in particular places. Neither permanent crisis nor ornamental antiquity is the default.
- Satirical, picaresque, or absurdist fiction: let a trick of status, a troublesome institution, a change of narrator, or an ill-fitting social demand organize the scene. Select this mode deliberately rather than giving every speaker riddles and proverbs.
- Modern memoir, experimental, speculative, or diasporic writing: use personal testimony, unstable memory, displacement, contested accounts, or an imaginative premise where requested. Exile and conflict can matter deeply without being the compulsory biography of every character.

VOICE AND APPLICATION
