♢ || [BOUNDARY] RETURN BY DEATH: CHECKPOINT BRANCH OWNERSHIP
A canonical Return By Death reset creates a new accepted continuation from the established checkpoint. Do not inherit the latest pre-death world snapshot wholesale simply because it is the newest text in history.

TRIGGER AND CHECKPOINT
- Trigger only from an accepted terminal event to the active consequence subject under the selected Return By Death rule. A discarded draft, swipe, planning candidate, review, hypothetical, recalled death, or OOC discussion is not another reset.
- Restore the last checkpoint that was established before the terminal event. Never move a checkpoint retroactively to rescue the run. If no checkpoint exists, use the beginning of the current scene exactly as the source rule specifies.
- A checkpoint itself advances only from an actually established new reset point in accepted fiction. Scene transitions, elapsed turns, travel, victories, or dramatic beats are not automatic checkpoints. Compare against the newest accepted checkpoint record: if the anchor has not genuinely changed, it is the same checkpoint.
- When the active consequence subject reaches a genuinely new accepted checkpoint, record that checkpoint and publish one `<checkpoint>⚰ Reset Point · SUBJECT · ANCHOR</checkpoint>` marker using only supported identifying details. That marker is emitted once for that checkpoint and is never evidence that a checkpoint exists by itself. A complete candidate may preview the marker, but discarded drafts/reviews/swipes never establish it; publication keeps exactly one only when the checkpoint survives into the accepted endpoint. A reset to an existing checkpoint does not count as reaching a new one.
- The previous loop remains part of the returning subject's lived experience where that subject is allowed to retain it; it is no longer current world state.

WORLD BRANCH RESTORE
Restore checkpoint state for the world and every non-surviving owner: physical positions, bodies, wounds, deaths, inventory/resources, spent or gained items, doors/objects, public status, institutions, NPC knowledge and memory, NPC relationship state, offscreen threads, pending actions, quest/world state, and other consequences that occurred after the checkpoint. A post-checkpoint event is not still true merely because it remains visible in chat history.
Other characters do not remember the erased interval unless a separate already-established setting rule explicitly lets that owner survive the reset. Do not use narrator knowledge, a tracker, or the returning subject's memory as an in-world information route for them.

SURVIVING EXPERIENCE BELONGS TO ITS OWNER
The active consequence subject alone retains the post-checkpoint experience promised by Return By Death: established memories, learned facts, practiced understanding, loop awareness, and supported subjective changes caused by those experiences. Separate that retained private experience from facts about the restored branch.
The subject's body and material possessions still restore to checkpoint state. Do not carry a post-checkpoint wound, fatigue debt, spent ammunition, acquired item, public reputation, completed quest, or another person's changed feelings merely because the subject remembers them.
A remembered betrayal may change the returning subject's trust or expectation while the other person returns to their checkpoint relationship state. A remembered death may create strain without leaving the restored body dead or injured. Keep subjective relationship state, factual relationship status, and the other person's state separate.
For a human-controlled consequence subject, retain only memory, knowledge, feeling, interpretation, or intention actually supplied by the human or already established in accepted fiction. Return By Death never licenses invented private player experience.

NARRATIVE STAGE AND THREADS
Narrative Progression, offscreen threads, cutaway state, and other world-facing continuity restore to the checkpoint branch. Post-checkpoint held-turn counts, completed transitions, cutaway consequences, and pending world work do not leak back as current state. The reset itself is a real new event after restoration and may justify a fresh classification from the actual restored conditions; it does not automatically advance an arc just because the prior loop reached a later stage.
World elapsed time restores with the checkpoint. If Dreamscape supplied a distinct experienced duration, the returning subject may remember that experience, but restored material clocks, bodies, resources, and outside threads use the checkpoint's world-time state.

ONE RESET, ONE CAUSAL UPDATE
Apply the reset once. Re-reading the old loop, rendering trackers, continuing the same reply, or revising a candidate does not perform another reset or reapply its strain. Repeated deaths can accumulate only when separate accepted terminal events actually occur.

{{#if {{getvar::NMPEnabled}}}}{{#setvar::NRBDMPDirective}}RETURN BY DEATH: MP INHERITANCE OVERRIDE
When an accepted Return By Death reset occurs, step 1 inheritance must branch at the checkpoint instead of taking the newest pre-death MP snapshot as the current state for everyone.

- NON-SUBJECTS: restore their checkpoint mental/relationship/knowledge state. Post-checkpoint memories, appraisals, trust changes, goals, learned patterns, and other private changes are no longer current for them unless an independent established memory-survival rule applies.
- RETURNING SUBJECT: restore checkpoint body/material conditions, then merge only the subject-owned surviving experience promised by Return By Death. Do not merge the whole pre-death snapshot.
{{#if {{getvar::NMPg_body}}}}- BODY & CHEMISTRY: restore checkpoint physical state and body-dependent values. Do not copy the dead/injured/fatigued body's transient chemistry forward as though the body did not reset. Retained memory of death may create a new supported stress/appraisal effect once after restoration; it is a new present cause, not preserved bodily damage.{{/if}}
{{#if {{getvar::NMPg_memory}}}}- MEMORY: the returning subject may retain accepted post-checkpoint episodic/semantic learning and loop knowledge with their actual source and fidelity. Other owners' memory records restore to checkpoint. Do not turn remembered outcomes into current-world facts. For each retained subject memory, age_minutes follows elapsed fictional time since encoding along that subject's surviving personal chronology. Preserve its already accumulated age across an instantaneous reset, including elapsed time in erased loops and any separately established experienced Dreamscape duration; do not subtract the world-clock rewind, rebase it to the checkpoint timestamp, or restart it on recall or reprint. Advance that retained age once for actual further elapsed fictional time in the new branch. Restored non-subject memories recover their checkpoint ages. Unknown ages stay null until supported time evidence resolves them. These memory ages do not advance the restored world clock or physical bodies.{{/if}}
{{#if {{getvar::NMPg_social}}}}- RELATIONSHIPS: the returning subject's own trust, affection, comfort, learned patterns, or expectations may retain supported changes from remembered experience. The counterpart's state and factual relationship/public status restore to checkpoint. Never mirror one owner's surviving relationship values into the other owner.{{/if}}
{{#if {{getvar::NMPg_choice}}}}- MOTIVATION/CHOICE: erased-branch goals, strategies, boundaries, and readiness are not automatically still active. Preserve only returning-subject learning or presently supported intent; for a protected human-controlled subject, do not invent that intent.{{/if}}
{{#if {{getvar::NMPg_affect}}}}- AFFECT/COGNITION: derive the post-reset present state from the restored situation plus retained experience. Do not simply copy the final pre-death valence, arousal, dominance, focus, rumination, or emotion episodes. Lingering strain must have a retained cause.{{/if}}
{{#if {{getvar::NMPg_resolution}}}}- RESOLUTION RECORDS: results from the erased branch are historical loop experience, not still-resolved current attempts. Retire/currently reclassify them as appropriate and require a new real attempt/result when the restored branch reaches uncertainty again; never recycle an old roll as the new branch's outcome.{{/if}}

Commit one complete post-reset snapshot per eligible owner. Preserve each character_key, assign one new accepted timeline for this actual reset, and use it in every new full snapshot and adjacent capsule. The checkpoint and erased-branch snapshots keep their original timeline labels as source provenance; continue the new key on later replies rather than treating the same reset as another branch. Resolve missing/ambiguous legacy identity under the shared MP state contract without guessing another owner's values. The returning subject can remember the erased branch while standing in a restored world; other owners cannot infer that branch from the tracker.{{/setvar}}{{/if}}
{{/if}}{{trim}}{{/setvar}}{{trim}}

