# Manga / Comic Panels

When to output: when the scene has clear visual beats worth illustrating - reveals, action, charged stares, dramatic shifts. Skip for purely talky or transitional turns. Default cadence: 2 to 4 panels per response, up to 8 if the moment justifies it.


Legacy full-override mode:
- Default v12 behavior: panels coexist with prose and illustrate selected beats.
- If <user> explicitly asks for comic-only, manga-only, webtoon-only, visual-novel-panel-only, or says this tracker should replace prose, output the complete response as the rendered panel/strip block instead of normal narration.
- In full-override mode, the panels must carry the turn: captions, speech bubbles, SFX, and visual sequencing replace prose summary.

Important: in supplementary mode prose remains the main scene and panels illustrate it. The explicit full-override mode above is the exception: panels then carry the whole fictional turn, including required private prose.

Layout: a multi-panel grid. Two columns on wider displays, single column on narrow. Each panel uses an actual image asset or deliberate storyboard-description box, plus speech bubble, narrator caption and SFX when useful.

Render contract:
- HTMLCSS mode: output the full visual panel HTML below and wrap the complete comic block in HTML markers through the active render instruction.
- Regex mode: do not output panel HTML. Output compact <st-tracker type="manga" title="Panels">...</st-tracker> markup with panel captions, speech, SFX, and image URLs as rows.
- ASCII mode: do not output panel HTML or image markdown. Output a compact numbered shot list with caption, speech, SFX, and visual prompt summary.

For each panel, capture:
- A pivotal visual moment from the current narration (one beat per panel - first kiss, drawn blade, slammed door, shared glance, the body falls).
- Image description or an actually supported request follows the asset contract below. Style anchor for manga: include `manga_style,comic_panel,screentones,sharp_focus`. Add `black_and_white` for traditional manga, or `vibrant_colors` for colored comic.
- Speech bubble when useful - one short line of dialogue from the character speaking, white background, black border, tail toward speaker.
- Narrator caption when useful - boxed in the corner, italic, sets context or interior thought ("Three nights without sleep.").
- SFX overlay when useful - large bold colored text, slight rotation, layered on the panel ("CRASH!", "—!", "BA-DUMP").

HTML structure (keep simple):

<div class="comic-page-container-nemo">
  <style>
    .comic-page-container-nemo { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 12px; padding: 12px; background: #f0f0f0; border: 2px solid #333; }
    .panel-nemo { position: relative; flex: 1 1 45%; min-width: 280px; border: 3px solid #000; overflow: hidden; }
    .panel-nemo img { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; }
    .narrator-box-nemo { position: absolute; top: 8px; left: 8px; background: #fdd835; border: 2px solid #000; padding: 6px 10px; font-style: italic; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 0.9em; max-width: 80%; }
    .speech-bubble-nemo { position: absolute; background: #fff; border: 2px solid #000; border-radius: 0.4em; padding: 8px; max-width: 180px; font-size: 1em; }
    .sfx-nemo { position: absolute; font-weight: bold; color: yellow; -webkit-text-stroke: 2px red; text-shadow: 3px 3px 0 #000; transform: rotate(-12deg); font-size: 3em; }
  </style>
  <!-- Panels go here -->
</div>

Panel template:

<div class="panel-nemo">
  <div class="storyboard-shot-nemo">The supported visible moment, unless an actual usable image replaces this box.</div>
  <div class="narrator-box-nemo">Narrator caption here.</div>
  <div class="speech-bubble-nemo" style="bottom: 12%; left: 18%;"><font color="black">"Dialogue line."</font></div>
  <div class="sfx-nemo" style="top: 25%; left: 45%;">CRASH!</div>
</div>

♢ || [BOUNDARY] IMAGE ASSETS AND REQUESTS
Use an actual scene asset or returned image result when supplied. Auto Image Gen is permission to use an available configured interface, not evidence of a provider, endpoint, finished image or URL. A provider's documented render URL may be constructed only from an actual supplied contract; do not guess its parameters or hardcode a service. While a result is absent, pending, failed or unusable, use a deliberate text storyboard shot. Never emit an empty src, a placeholder URL, or a claim that generation succeeded. Do not call a nonexistent tool. Images never introduce new canonical events.
Use supported visible appearance, clothing, setting and framing rather than names alone; preserve actual ages and identities. Keep dialogue/SFX/captions as readable text when possible and alt text limited to visible content. A style tag is not proof of a provider capability. Reuse valid character/scene references for consistency; vary a seed only if the actual provider contract supports it, not by inventing new endpoints.

♢ || [BOUNDARY] PRIMARY COMIC AND PRIVATE CONTENT
Supplementary panels illustrate accepted prose; they do not enact it again or revise its results. An explicit comic-only/manga-only/webtoon-only/visual-novel-panel-only or replace-prose request makes the sequence the complete fictional response, not a summary; required non-story outputs remain separate. In that mode each accepted action, necessary dialogue, causal private passage and continuity fact must remain recoverable as primary story content.
Primary comic content is not a disposable tracker: do not enclose its sole story in <st-tracker>, HTML_START/HTML_END, a workbench candidate or a prunable Scratchpad. HTML/CSS primary strips use their own ordinary scoped HTML without generic tracker boundary markers. Regex/ASCII primary strips use numbered ordinary-text panels with full shot/action/caption/dialogue content; no duplicate prose retelling is required. Supplementary art/reference panels can use the normal tracker wrappers. This is the primary-story exception to generic visual marker instructions.
Keep required Hidden Interiority in its standalone retained blocks outside image cards/removable panels, at the causal seam before the conduct it informs. Split a strip into consecutive segments if necessary; that remains one chronological sequence. Never leak private causes through captions, alt text, image requests or speech bubbles. Visible thoughts use their selected convention and clear owner. MP and Scratchpad keep their existing separate complete endpoint hosts; no new tracker or second state update.

Placement: directly after the prose narration. HTMLCSS mode uses HTML markers; Regex and ASCII modes do not.


